I did a more thorough analysis of the question back in one of the old
issues of Ju'i Lobypli, in response to a question from University of
Maryland professor James Yorke, and included a variety of examples of=20
how it might work (or not work, as you conclude).  Alas, the current=20
website search doesn't seem to find things in the old JLs, which are=20
somewhere on the website, and I am not finding it immediately in my own=20
copies.

A better example to respond to the questioner might be to take a=20
paragraph of Lojban text, say from Alice, and simply substitute English=20
keywords for the gismu, and hyphenated-keywords for the lujvo.  It still=20
doesn't work, but I think it is closer to what is being asked.

> i lu uo sei la alis pensi mi ba lo nu farlu tai ti cu ba na=92e xanka le =
nu farlu
> fo le serti i lu ua virnu sei le lanzu tu=92a mi ba jinvi li=92u i u=92o =
mi noda cusku
> va=92o ji=92asai le nu mi farlu fi le drudi be le zdani tosa=92a la=92e d=
i=92u la=92a jetnu toi
> li=92u

Using English gismu, this becomes:

i lu uo sei la alis thinks mi ba lo nu fall tai ti cu ba na=92e nervous le=
=20
nu fall fo le stairs i lu ua brave sei le family tu=92a mi ba opines li=92u=
=20
i u=92o mi noda express va=92o ji=92asai le nu mi fall fi le roof be le nes=
t=20
tosa=92a la=92e di=92u la=92a true toi li=92u

I think it pretty safe to say that few would find this any easier to=20
understand than the pure Lojban, even discounting the confusion caused=20
by Lojban gismu not meaning exactly the same thing as the keywords.

